      that is what one raw point can be wrong by.
    form check, ADDDMAREG burst, cascade vs loop where both run:
             n    cascade       loop  loop-casc
            16         38         32         -6
            32         65         52        -13
            64        480        313       -167
           128       1071        889       -182
      -> over n=16..128: cascade 9.223, loop 7.652 cycles per instruction
         (difference -1.571 -- the loop's own back edge is in this, and
         `q_loop_addi` above says how much of it that is)
    out to n=1024:  issuing core 8.850  |  drained 9.018  |  issue-limited baseline 1.126 (`q_loop_addi`)
    backlog in flight when the last .ttinsn returned (cycles of ThCon
    work, less 90 for tensix_sync()'s own cost at n=16):
      n=16 +0  n=32 +142  n=64 +163  n=128 +169  n=256 +151  n=512 +163  n=1024 +169  
    marginal cost between adjacent burst lengths:  16->32 1.25  32->64 8.16  64->128 9.00  128->256 9.00  256->512 9.00  512->1024 9.00  
    -> KNEE between n=32 and n=64: the marginal reaches 8.16 against a
       drained 9.018, so the core is back-pressured from there on.
    -> the backlog FLATTENED at ~169 cycles (last two doublings added
       12 then 6), clearing a 38-cycle noise floor, and at the
       drained rate of 9.018 cycles per instruction that is ~19
       INSTRUCTIONS in flight -- a measurement of the Tensix
       instruction queue's depth in entries.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
phase F: does a bigger loop body cost more per instruction?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Same instruction throughout; only the distance between the loop's two ends
  changes. The ISA docs give the fetch PERIOD (one 128-bit L1 read per four
  instructions) and no cache size and no miss cost, so a flat row is
  consistent with the documentation and a cliff is a number nothing has
  published. Both are results.

  THIS PHASE'S BODIES ARE FROZEN. Two tracked silicon datasets carry its
  rows, and its build is within a few hundred bytes of tt-metal's kernel
  config buffer besides. The footprints BETWEEN 1024 and 2048, which narrow
  the boundary this phase located, are phase G.

